# Suppose we are building a medical appointment scheduling web application.

# Frontend
# The frontend is the part users interact with.
# Examples in the application:
# 1-Appointment booking form
# 2-Login page
# 3-Calendar with available times
# 4-Patient dashboard

# Possible Frontend Technologies
# 1-HTML
# 2-CSS
# 3-JavaScript
# 4-React (framework of JS)
# 5-Angular (framework of JS)
# 6-Vue.js (framework of JS)

# Backend
# The backend handles the logic and data of the system.

# Examples:
# 1-Storing appointments in a database
# 2-Authenticating users
# 3-Managing doctor availability
# 4-Sending appointment confirmations

# Possible Backend Technologies
# 1-Node.js
# 2-Python (Django / Flask)
# 3-Java (Spring Boot)

# Databases
# 1-PostgreSQL
# 2-MySQL
# 3-MongoDB

# Communication Between Frontend and Backend
# Frontend communicates with backend through APIs.

# Process:
# 1-The frontend sends an HTTP request.
# 2-The request reaches an API endpoint.
# 3-The backend processes the request.
# 4-The backend returns a response (usually JSON).

# Example:
# 1-Frontend request:
# 2-GET /api/appointments

# Backend response:
# {
#  "doctor": "Dr. Garcia",
#  "date": "2026-03-15",
#  "time": "10:30"
# }

# Communication model:
# Frontend → HTTP Request → API → Backend
# Backend → HTTP Response → Frontend
